instruction:
Goal: fix a bug in /echo/bind.go (Go module github.com/labstack/echo/v4, repo root /echo). ONLY edit /echo/bind.go. Do not touch any test file.

BUG: `func (b *DefaultBinder) bindData(destination interface{}, data map[string][]string, tag string) error` only supports struct destinations. Binding into a map destination must be supported (hidden test `TestDefaultBinder_bindDataToMap`, subtest "ok, bind to map[string][]string"). Currently a map destination panics (reflect: NumField of non-struct type).

Required behaviour (implement inside bindData, right after `typ := reflect.TypeOf(destination).Elem()` and `val := reflect.ValueOf(destination).Elem()`):

	// Support binding to limited Map destinations:
	// - map[string][]string,
	// - map[string]string <-- (binds first value from data slice)
	// - map[string]interface{}
	// - map[string]int
	// - map[string]bool
	// - map[string]float64
	// - map[string]int64
	// - map[string]float32
	// - map[string]int32
	// - map[string]uint
	// - map[string]uint32
	// - map[string]uint64
	// - map[string]uint8
	// - map[string]int8
	// - map[string]int16
	// - map[string]uint16
	// - map[string]string
	// - map[string]time.Time

Semantics:
- Trigger only when `typ.Kind() == reflect.Map && typ.Key().Kind() == reflect.String`.
- Supported element kinds: slice, string, bool, all int kinds, all uint kinds, all float kinds, interface, and time.Time (compare with `reflect.TypeOf(time.Time{})`). Anything else (e.g. struct{}, map, ptr, chan, func) must return an error: `errors.New("binding to map with type " + typ.Elem().String() + " is not supported")` (so for map[string]struct{} the message is exactly `binding to map with type struct {} is not supported`).
- If the destination map is nil (`val.IsNil()`), initialize it with `val.Set(reflect.MakeMap(typ))`.
- For each key/values pair in data:
  * element kind slice: build a new slice of length len(values) via reflect.MakeSlice and set each element with the existing helper `setWithProperType(elemKind, v, slice.Index(i))` (this makes map[string][]string work and also map[string][]int); if setWithProperType returns an error, return it.
  * element kind interface: `val.SetMapIndex(reflect.ValueOf(key), reflect.ValueOf(values))` (bind the whole []string slice).
  * otherwise: create `elem := reflect.New(typ.Elem()).Elem()`, set it with `setWithProperType(typ.Elem().Kind(), values[0], elem)` (returns error if parsing fails), then `val.SetMapIndex(reflect.ValueOf(key), elem)`. For time.Time use the existing helper path used for struct fields (setWithProperType handles BindUnmarshaler/TextUnmarshaler via unmarshalFieldNonPtr when the kind is struct/interface — check the existing code and make map[string]time.Time work by parsing RFC3339 values; if setWithProperType cannot handle it, special-case time.Time by calling `elem.Addr().Interface().(encoding.TextUnmarshaler).UnmarshalText([]byte(values[0]))`).
- After filling the map, `return nil` (do NOT fall through to the struct-field loop).
- Do not change any other behaviour of bindData (struct binding, tags, etc.).

Verification (report raw output of each):
- `cd /echo && gofmt -l bind.go` (must print nothing)
- `cd /echo && go build ./... && go vet ./...`
- `cd /echo && go test -count=1 ./...` (all pre-existing tests must still pass)
- Create a TEMPORARY test file /echo/zz_tmp_bindmap_test.go (package echo) with a table test calling `b := new(DefaultBinder); err := b.bindData(&dest, input, "query")` for: map[string][]string ({"a":{"1","2"},"b":{"3"}} -> same), map[string]string (-> {"a":"1","b":"3"}), map[string]interface{} (-> {"a":[]string{"1","2"},"b":[]string{"3"}}), map[string]int (-> {"a":1,"b":3}), map[string]bool, map[string]float64, map[string]time.Time (RFC3339 value), map[string]struct{} (expect error "binding to map with type struct {} is not supported"), and a nil map destination (map[string][]string(nil) -> initialized and filled). Run `cd /echo && go test -count=1 -run TestTmpBindMap -v ./`, t
